WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:03.160
Can the Libertarian Party
become a major contender

00:00:03.160 --> 00:00:05.810
that consistently wins elections?

00:00:05.810 --> 00:00:09.410
If the organization's
dismal 50-year track record

00:00:09.410 --> 00:00:11.240
isn't discouraging enough,

00:00:11.240 --> 00:00:15.610
now the LP is in disarray after
its chairman and two members

00:00:15.610 --> 00:00:18.980
of its National Committee
resigned in the wake of an attempt

00:00:18.980 --> 00:00:22.030
to de-certify the New Hampshire affiliate

00:00:22.030 --> 00:00:24.970
due to conflicts over its
social media presence.

00:00:24.970 --> 00:00:28.840
This episode has revealed an
organization at war with itself

00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:31.580
over vision, tactics, and messaging.

00:00:31.580 --> 00:00:33.750
To get a better sense of what's going on,

00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:37.180
Reason spoke with former Michigan
Congressman Justin Amash,

00:00:37.180 --> 00:00:39.530
who finished his five terms in office

00:00:39.530 --> 00:00:43.690
as the first and only libertarian
to hold national office.

00:00:43.690 --> 00:00:45.660
He had been elected as a Republican,

00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:49.020
became an independent,
and affiliated with the LP

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:51.040
for the last few months of his tenure.

00:00:51.040 --> 00:00:54.620
Amash flirted with a run for
the LPs presidential nomination

00:00:54.620 --> 00:00:58.280
in 2020 before choosing to
drop out of consideration.

00:00:58.280 --> 00:01:00.550
Despite not holding an official position

00:01:00.550 --> 00:01:03.900
in the party's leadership,
he is its best known member.

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:05.760
In the wake of the recent scandal,

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:08.010
he chastised the New Hampshire affiliate

00:01:08.010 --> 00:01:11.840
for what he called its horrible
messaging even as he said

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:15.100
attempts to remove its
leadership were illegitimate.

00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:17.620
I'm committed to making
the Libertarian Party

00:01:17.620 --> 00:01:18.710
a major contender

00:01:18.710 --> 00:01:21.450
that consistently wins
elections, he wrote.

00:01:21.450 --> 00:01:25.080
We must work together to
build a big tent party

00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:26.990
that can take on the old parties

00:01:26.990 --> 00:01:29.523
and defend the rights of the people.

00:01:30.580 --> 00:01:34.210
Representative Justin Amash,
thanks for talking to Reason.

00:01:34.210 --> 00:01:35.440
Thanks, Nick.

00:01:35.440 --> 00:01:38.290
So you've written and
you've talked about the LP

00:01:38.290 --> 00:01:41.520
as you see it or you insist

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:43.720
that it should be a major contender

00:01:43.720 --> 00:01:45.980
that consistently wins elections.

00:01:45.980 --> 00:01:47.860
How are you feeling about that right now

00:01:47.860 --> 00:01:50.680
in the wake of this resignation

00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:53.910
of the Libertarian Party's
national chairperson

00:01:53.910 --> 00:01:55.160
as well as two members

00:01:55.160 --> 00:01:57.143
of the Libertarian National Committee?

00:01:58.270 --> 00:02:00.040
I still feel positive.

00:02:00.040 --> 00:02:01.020
It's a small party.

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:05.410
So when you have a small
party, you can have things

00:02:05.410 --> 00:02:07.970
get upset pretty quickly
and pretty easily.

00:02:07.970 --> 00:02:11.810
You know, a few people
shake up one state affiliate

00:02:11.810 --> 00:02:15.490
and all of a sudden, a lot
of things start to crumble.

00:02:15.490 --> 00:02:17.370
So it's not that surprising

00:02:17.370 --> 00:02:18.850
when you have a party of this size,

00:02:18.850 --> 00:02:23.200
but my goal is to make the
party much, much larger.

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:24.950
And when you have a much larger party,

00:02:24.950 --> 00:02:27.650
things like this just
don't have the same impact.

00:02:27.650 --> 00:02:29.820
I mean, when you look
at the Republican Party

00:02:29.820 --> 00:02:33.170
or the Democratic Party, they
have crazy stuff going on

00:02:33.170 --> 00:02:35.730
all the time and the
party doesn't fall apart

00:02:35.730 --> 00:02:38.340
because it's big enough and robust enough.

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:40.670
So that's kind of a success story, right?

00:02:40.670 --> 00:02:42.360
Their parties fall apart

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:46.060
because they are rife
with liars and hypocrites.

00:02:46.060 --> 00:02:49.223
The Libertarian Party falls
apart because people disagree.

00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:52.550
Yeah, I mean, it's small.

00:02:52.550 --> 00:02:55.440
It's not that the party is falling apart.

00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:58.290
It's that the party doesn't
really have an identity yet.

00:02:58.290 --> 00:02:59.123
Okay.

00:02:59.123 --> 00:03:00.870
You know, it doesn't
have a clear identity.

00:03:02.180 --> 00:03:04.330
Let's talk about that in a second.

00:03:04.330 --> 00:03:07.120
I want to ask, in a tweet that came out

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:11.220
right after all of this stuff
between the national party

00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:14.240
and particularly things going
on in the Libertarian Party

00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:17.210
of New Hampshire came out, you wrote

00:03:17.210 --> 00:03:21.490
I think what was a perfectly
balanced kind of discussion.

00:03:21.490 --> 00:03:24.980
You said that there was a due
process violation on the part

00:03:24.980 --> 00:03:28.493
of the national party or some
people in the national party.

00:03:30.040 --> 00:03:34.560
What due process got mucked up in this?

00:03:37.151 --> 00:03:39.270
Well, it didn't seem to me
that you could just go off

00:03:39.270 --> 00:03:40.680
and start a new state affiliate

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:44.490
and claim that it was the
legitimate state affiliate

00:03:44.490 --> 00:03:46.997
without going through proper channels.

00:03:46.997 --> 00:03:50.330
You can't just have one
state party chairman

00:03:51.240 --> 00:03:52.600
go off and do that.

00:03:52.600 --> 00:03:56.940
And it doesn't reflect
on my opinion of, like,

00:03:56.940 --> 00:03:59.603
do I agree with the chairman
philosophically or not?

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:06.060
I had plenty of concerns about
the way the Libertarian Party

00:04:06.060 --> 00:04:08.250
of New Hampshire was running
its social media accounts

00:04:08.250 --> 00:04:10.760
and other things that were going on.

00:04:10.760 --> 00:04:11.930
But at the same time,

00:04:11.930 --> 00:04:14.000
you have to go through the proper process.

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:16.860
And if we don't like what's going on

00:04:16.860 --> 00:04:18.870
with the state affiliate, the thing you do

00:04:18.870 --> 00:04:22.105
is bring it to the attention
of the party at large.

00:04:22.105 --> 00:04:24.110
You might bring it to the attention

00:04:24.110 --> 00:04:27.160
of the Libertarian National Committee.

00:04:27.160 --> 00:04:28.610
You definitely bring it to attention

00:04:28.610 --> 00:04:31.760
of people within the state,
but you don't just run off

00:04:31.760 --> 00:04:33.630
and form a new state
affiliate and say, well,

00:04:33.630 --> 00:04:36.310
this is the new party and we're gonna also

00:04:36.310 --> 00:04:39.070
take the resources, by
the way, of the old party

00:04:39.070 --> 00:04:40.570
and lock out the executive committee

00:04:40.570 --> 00:04:42.410
that was elected at the previous election.

00:04:42.410 --> 00:04:44.745
So in the same statement about it,

00:04:44.745 --> 00:04:48.400
you talked about horrible
messaging and edgelording

00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:50.460
coming from people running,

00:04:50.460 --> 00:04:52.040
particularly the social media account

00:04:52.040 --> 00:04:54.350
of the Libertarian Party of New Hampshire.

00:04:54.350 --> 00:04:55.630
What were the examples of that?

00:04:55.630 --> 00:04:59.283
or what were the horrible
messages that were being said?

00:05:00.140 --> 00:05:05.140
Oh, there was one about
John McCain's brain tumor.

00:05:05.790 --> 00:05:10.790
Right, yeah, McCain's brain
tumor saved more lives

00:05:11.090 --> 00:05:12.537
than Anthony Fauci, something like that.

00:05:12.537 --> 00:05:14.580
Yeah, something like that.

00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:16.790
There was another one about child labor.

00:05:16.790 --> 00:05:18.850
There were a lot of examples,

00:05:18.850 --> 00:05:22.350
and the person who was
allegedly doing the tweets

00:05:22.350 --> 00:05:25.400
through the party account was also

00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:30.290
putting out some pretty crazy
personal tweets as well.

00:05:30.290 --> 00:05:34.810
So, you know, there was a
lot of horrible messaging.

